{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P51587","secondaryAccessions":["O00183","O15008","Q13879","Q5TBJ7"],"uniProtkbId":"BRCA2_HUMAN","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"1996-10-01","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-09-02","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"2022-02-23","entryVersion":252,"sequenceVersion":4},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Homo sapiens","commonName":"Human","taxonId":9606,"lineage":["Eukaryota","Metazoa","Chordata","Craniata","Vertebrata","Euteleostomi","Mammalia","Eutheria","Euarchontoglires","Primates","Haplorrhini","Catarrhini","Hominidae","Homo"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"8524414"}],"value":"Breast cancer type 2 susceptibility protein"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"15199141"}],"value":"Fanconi anemia group D1 protein"}}]},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"8524414"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000312","source":"HGNC","id":"HGNC:1101"}],"value":"BRCA2"},"synonyms":[{"value":"FACD"},{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"15199141"}],"value":"FANCD1"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11239456"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12442171"}],"value":"Tumor suppressor protein that maintains genome stability primarily by repairing damaged DNA through homologous recombination (HR) (PubMed:11239456, PubMed:12442171, PubMed:15115758, PubMed:15199141, PubMed:15671039, PubMed:15937124, PubMed:17515903, PubMed:17515904, PubMed:18317453, PubMed:19303847, PubMed:20729832, PubMed:20729858, PubMed:20729859, PubMed:21719596, PubMed:27941124, PubMed:37499663, PubMed:37515771). Facilitates the repair of double-strand breaks (DSBs) by binding and mediating the loading of the RAD51 protein onto single-stranded DNA (ssDNA), thereby promoting the activity of RAD51, which catalyzes DNA strand exchange (PubMed:11239456, PubMed:12442171, PubMed:15937124, PubMed:17515903, PubMed:17515904, PubMed:18317453, PubMed:19303847, PubMed:20729832, PubMed:20729858, PubMed:20729859, PubMed:27941124, PubMed:37499663). BRCA2 nucleates and stabilizes RAD51 on ssDNA directly and delivers RAD51 to ssDNA-double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) junctions by sliding along dsDNA backbone (PubMed:12442171, PubMed:19303847, PubMed:37499663). RAD51 targeting to ssDNA promotes removal of replication protein-A (RPA) from ssDNA and stabilization of RAD51-ssDNA filaments by blocking ATP hydrolysis (PubMed:20729859). May play a role in the extension step after strand invasion at replication-dependent DNA double-strand breaks; together with PALB2 is involved in both POLH localization at collapsed replication forks and DNA polymerization activity (PubMed:24485656). Required to prevent R-loop-associated DNA damage and thus transcription-associated genomic instability (PubMed:24896180). Silencing of BRCA2 promotes R-loop accumulation at actively transcribed genes in replicating and non-replicating cells, suggesting that BRCA2 mediates the control of R-loop associated genomic instability, independently of its known role in homologous recombination (PubMed:24896180). Also promotes RAD51 loading to telomeric regions, facilitating telomere replication and capping (PubMed:21076401). Also required for homologous recombination during meiosis by promoting the recruitment of RAD51 and DMC1 recombinases to meiotic DSB sites, enabling proper chromosome pairing and crossing over (PubMed:26976601). Also promotes homologous recombination by inactivating the FIGNL1-FIRRM complex to protect RAD51 filament from premature disassembly (PubMed:37515771). Together with NPM1, may also regulate centrosome duplication (PubMed:21084279)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","note":{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"26833090"}],"value":"Colocalizes with ERCC5/XPG to nuclear foci following DNA replication stress"}]},"subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11239456"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"19303847"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"37499663"}],"value":"Chromosome","id":"SL-0468"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21076401"}],"value":"Chromosome, telomere","id":"SL-0276"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"24013206"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"26833090"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"21276791"}],"value":"Nucleus","id":"SL-0191"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"21276791"}],"value":"Cytoplasm, cytoskeleton, microtubule organizing center, centrosome","id":"SL-0048"}}]},{"commentType":"DISEASE","disease":{"diseaseId":"Breast cancer","diseaseAccession":"DI-02602","acronym":"BC","description":"A common malignancy originating from breast epithelial tissue. Breast neoplasms can be distinguished by their histologic pattern. Invasive ductal carcinoma is by far the most common type. Breast cancer is etiologically and genetically heterogeneous. Important genetic factors have been indicated by familial occurrence and bilateral involvement. Mutations at more than one locus can be involved in different families or even in the same case.","diseaseCrossReference":{"database":"MIM","id":"114480"},"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10399947"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10978364"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11139248"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11149425"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11241844"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11948477"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12145750"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12373604"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12442274"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12442275"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12569143"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"12938098"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"14722926"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15026808"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15172753"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"15365993"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16793542"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"17924331"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"23108138"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"24013206"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29161300"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"8640237"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9150152"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9609997"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9654203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9971877"}]},"note":{"texts":[{"value":"Disease susceptibility is associated with variants affecting the gene represented in this entry"}]}},{"commentType":"DISEASE","disease":{"diseaseId":"Pancreatic cancer 2","diseaseAccession":"DI-02847","acronym":"PNCA2","description":"A malignant neoplasm of the pancreas. Tumors can arise from both the exocrine and endocrine portions of the pancreas, but 95% of them develop from the exocrine portion, including the ductal epithelium, acinar cells, connective tissue, and lymphatic tissue.","diseaseCrossReference":{"database":"MIM","id":"613347"},"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9140390"}]},"note":{"texts":[{"value":"The disease is caused by variants affecting the gene represented in this entry"}]}},{"commentType":"DISEASE","disease":{"diseaseId":"Breast-ovarian cancer, familial, 2","diseaseAccession":"DI-02603","acronym":"BROVCA2","description":"A condition associated with familial predisposition to cancer of the breast and ovaries. Characteristic features in affected families are an early age of onset of breast cancer (often before age 50), increased chance of bilateral cancers (cancer that develop in both breasts, or both ovaries, independently), frequent occurrence of breast cancer among men, increased incidence of tumors of other specific organs, such as the prostate.","diseaseCrossReference":{"database":"MIM","id":"612555"},"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10584720"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"11158174"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7658781"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"9150155"}]},"note":{"texts":[{"value":"Disease susceptibility is associated with variants affecting the gene represented in this entry"}]}}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":3418,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Breast cancer type 2 susceptibility protein","featureId":"PRO_0000064984"},{"type":"Repeat","location":{"start":{"value":1002,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1036,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"BRCA2 1"},{"type":"Repeat","location":{"start":{"value":1212,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1246,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"BRCA2 2"},{"type":"Repeat","location":{"start":{"value":1421,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1455,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"BRCA2 3"},{"type":"Repeat","location":{"start":{"value":1517,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":1551,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"BRCA2 4"},{"type":"Repeat","location":{"start":{"value":1